Chromium's own Vulkan implementation for HarmonyOS.

The layer under ANGLE: what viz and Skia use when the GPU is driven by
Vulkan. Modelled on gpu/vulkan/android, which is the nearest relative --
a windowing system reached through a platform surface extension and a
loader called libvulkan.so.

Two things had to be said before it could exist at all. features.gni
offered Vulkan to linux, chromeos, android and fuchsia, and OHOS is none
of those, so enable_vulkan could not be turned on here even by asking; it
now can be, and is still off unless asked. And the factory in
gpu/vulkan/init never reaches its BUILDFLAG(IS_ANDROID) ladder on an
Ozone platform -- Ozone is asked first -- so the implementation is handed
back by the OHOS surface factory, where this port's other platform
choices already are.

Buffer sharing is not implemented. Android does it through
AHardwareBuffer; HarmonyOS has VK_OHOS_native_buffer and
VK_OHOS_external_memory for the same purpose and that is its own piece of
work. CanImportGpuMemoryBuffer answers no rather than half doing it.

Two more things only a compiler knew. gpu/vulkan/vulkan_export.h does not
exist in 154 -- components say COMPONENT_EXPORT(NAME) now -- and
vkCreateSurfaceOHOS is not among Chromium's Vulkan function pointers,
which are generated from the Khronos headers and so have never heard of
this platform either.

That second one is the same thing the ANGLE backend hit, and it will be
the same again for VK_OHOS_native_buffer and VK_OHOS_external_memory when
buffer sharing is wired up: an extension the SDK declares and the device's
loader exports, but the headers this is built against do not carry. It is
asked for by name, with the structure repeated locally.


